            is a bijection.
          %h3#interpretation Interpretation
          %p For emphasis, here is the interpretation of these three corollaries in words:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %strong corollary I
                says that the interpretation of presheaves on
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                as generalized objects probeable by objects
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi c
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
                of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                is consistent: the probes of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                by
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi c
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
                are indeed the maps of generalized objects from
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi c
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
                into
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %li
              %p
                %strong corollary II
                says that probes by objects of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                are sufficient to distinguish objects of
                = succeed ":" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi C
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                are the same if they have the same probes by other objects of
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi C
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %li
              %p
                %strong corollary III
                characterizes
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representable+functor"} representable functors
                by a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+property"} universal property
                and is hence the bridge between the notion of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representable+functor"} representable functor
                and
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+construction"} universal constructions
          %h2#generalizations Generalizations
          %p
            The Yoneda lemma tends to carry over to all important generalizations of the context of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+small+category"} categories
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                There is an analog of the Yoneda lemma in
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+category+theory"} enriched category theory
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+Yoneda+lemma"} enriched Yoneda lemma
            %li
              %p
                In the context of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/module"} modules
                (see also
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Day+convolution"} Day convolution
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+reduction"} Yoneda reduction
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi hom
                        %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo ,
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hom_C(-, -)
                as a unit bimodule.
            %li
              %p
                There is a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+lemma+for+bicategories"} Yoneda lemma for bicategories
            %li
              %p
                There is a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+lemma+for+tricategories"} Yoneda lemma for tricategories
            %li
              %p
                There is a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+lemma+for+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} Yoneda lemma for (∞,1)-categories
            %li
              %p
                In
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functional+programming"} functional programming
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuation+passing+style"} continuation passing style
                transform.
            %li
              %p
                Formulation of the lemma in
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
                %a{:href => "http://homotopytypetheory.org/2012/05/02/a-type-theoretical-yoneda-lemma/"} A type theoretical Yoneda lemma
                at
                %a{:href => "http://homotopytypetheory.org"} homotopytypetheory.org
          %h2#necessity_of_naturality Necessity of naturality
          %p
            The assumption of naturality is necessary for the Yoneda lemma to hold. A simple counter-example is given by a category with two objects
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Hom
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi Hom
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi Hom
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi B
                    %mo ,
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi ℤ
                      %mrow
                        %mo ≥
                        %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Hom(A,A) = Hom(A,B) = Hom(B,B) = \mathbb{Z}_{\geq 0}
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Hom
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi B
                    %mo ,
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi ℤ
                      %mrow
                        %mo ≥
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Hom(B,A) = \mathbb{Z}_{\geq 1}
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Hom(A,-)
            is isomorphic to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi B
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Hom(B,-)
            for any choice of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} -
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            are not isomorphic (composition with any arrow
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mo →
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B \rightarrow A
            is greater than or equal to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            is the identity on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            and
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
          %p
            A finite counter-example is given by the category with two objects
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Hom
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi Hom
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi Hom
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi B
                    %mo ,
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Hom(A,A) = Hom(A,B) = Hom(B,B) = \{0, 1\}
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Hom
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi B
                    %mo ,
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Hom(B,A) = \{0, 2\}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Hom(A,-)
            is isomorphic to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi B
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Hom(B,-)
            for any choice of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} -
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            are not isomorphic (composition with any arrow
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mo →
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B \rightarrow A
            is
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            is the identity on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            and
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
          %p
            On the other hand, there have been examples of locally finite categories where naturality is not necessary. For example,
            %a{:href => "#Lovasz1967"} Lovász 1967, Theorem 3.6 (iv)
            states precisely that finite relational structures
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            are isomorphic if, and only if,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo ,
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≅
                  %mi Hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo ,
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Hom(C,A) \cong Hom(C,B)
            for every finite relational structure
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %a{:href => "#Pultr1973"} Pultr 1973, Theorem 2.2
            generalised the result to finitely well-powered, locally finite categories with (extremal epi, mono)
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/factorization+system"} factorization system
          %h2#the_yoneda_lemma_in_semicategories The Yoneda lemma in semicategories
          %p
            An interesting phenomenon arises in the case of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semicategory"} semicategories
            i.e. “categories” (possibly) lacking
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+morphisms"} identity morphisms
          %p
            the Yoneda lemma fails in general, since its validity in a semicategory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒢
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{G}
            implies that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒢
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{G}
            is in fact already a category because the Yoneda lemma permits to embed
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒢
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{G}
            into
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi PrSh
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝒢
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} PrSh(\mathcal{G})
            and the latter is always a category, the embedding then implying that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒢
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{G}
            is itself a category!
          %p
            But for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+semicategories"} regular semicategories
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℛ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{R}
            there is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unity+of+opposites"} unity of opposites
            in the category of all
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semipresheaves"} semipresheaves
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℛ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{R}
            between the so called regular presheaves that are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimits"} colimits
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representable+presheaf"} representables
            and presheaves satisfying the Yoneda lemma, whence
            %em the Yoneda lemma holds dialectically for regular presheaves!
          %p
            For some of the details see at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+semicategory"} regular semicategory
            and the references therein.
          %h2#applications Applications
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The Yoneda lemma is the or a central ingredient in various
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reconstruction+theorem"} reconstruction theorems
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tannaka+duality"} Tannaka duality
            %li
              %p
                In its incarnations as
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+reduction"} Yoneda reduction
                the Yoneda lemma governs the algebra of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/end"} ends
                and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coend"} coends
                and hence that of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bimodule"} bimodules
                and
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/profunctor"} profunctors
            %li
              %p
                The Yoneda lemma is effectively the reason that
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Isbell+conjugation"} Isbell conjugation
                exists. This is a fundamental duality that relates
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry"} geometry
                and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra"} algebra
                in large part of mathematics.
          %h2#related_entries Related entries
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+reduction"} Yoneda reduction
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/co-Yoneda+lemma"} co-Yoneda lemma
            %li
